{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CfZzUrBqCyAe2LqgxWdT1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe endpoint decomposition is fine — a dedicated `OwnerCreatedEvent` value type, built from the owner and emitted by the existing `AuditOwnerCreated` step, is the right place for this, and the `seq` source lives with the event it numbers. The `primaryIdentifier` abstraction is a reasonable nod to the spec's memberId evolution. But two structural issues:\n\n1. **Hand-rolled JSON serialization reinvents Jackson.** `toJson()` plus the 30-line `quote()` helper — manual string escaping, control-char `\\uXXXX` encoding, the works — is effectively a bespoke JSON string encoder living inside a domain event class. This project already serializes everything through Jackson (`ObjectResponse<T>`, the DTOs). A maintainer here would expect a structured event to be produced the same way, not via a private char-by-char escaper. This is logic in the wrong unit: the event record has quietly become a mini JSON library. Prefer a shared/static `ObjectMapper` (the record can carry `@JsonProperty` names, or build a small `Map`), which is both idiomatic and correct-by-construction, and drops `quote()` entirely.\n\n2. **The `primaryIdentifier` abstraction is only half-delivered, creating an internal naming mismatch.** The record component is `primaryIdentifier`, but `of(Owner)` hardcodes `getCustomerCode()` and `toJson()` hardcodes the literal key `\"customerCode\"`. So the same concept is named three different ways within one ~87-line class, and the abstraction that motivated the field name doesn't actually reach the serialized output. When the memberId migration lands, a maintainer still has to hand-edit both the factory and the string literal — the abstract name buys almost nothing while costing clarity. Either commit to a concrete `customerCode` field (matches today's spec, simpler) or route the JSON key through the same abstraction (e.g. a field/`@JsonProperty` name that moves with the identifier) so the name and the wire form stay in sync.\n\nNeither is a correctness issue; both are about the event class carrying more (and more inconsistent) structure than a maintainer of this codebase would find natural."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11062,"cache_read_input_tokens":11894,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11062},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d395cda9-0c6e-408d-a08d-cd68e9719988","uuid":"82f75527-acbf-4a89-bba8-6973f85d14f3","timestamp":"2026-09-30T17:08:57.134Z","request_id":"req_011CfZzUqsErsRLx2Afe3A1b"}
